## Migration: `BackgroundProcessor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Analysis

Your code has **three** patterns that won't work in .NET 8:

| Pattern Found | Line | Issue in .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `Stop()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `ProcessLoop()` | Never thrown — cleanup logic silently lost |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | `ProcessLoop()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### ⚠️ Key Pitfalls in This Migration

1. **`GetNextItem()` may block** — if it blocks indefinitely, `CancellationToken` alone won't wake it. You must pass the token into that method or use an interruptible alternative.
2. **`RollbackCurrentTransaction()` cleanup is hidden in a catch block** — if you just delete the catch, you lose rollback logic. It must move to a cancellation-aware path.
3. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` in `Stop()` is a code smell** — replace with `Thread.Join(timeout)` for a proper timed wait.

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — roll back any partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop (replaces Thread.ResetAbort + loop check)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // In .NET 8 there is no way to force-kill a managed thread.
            // If this happens, GetNextItem/ProcessItem must be fixed to
            // check the CancellationToken more frequently.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot wake a blocked thread |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal instead of forcible injection |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | The exception type thrown by `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` and cancellation-aware APIs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | `break` | You control the loop — just exit it |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2000)` | Actually waits for the thread instead of hoping it finishes |
| — | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed to avoid timer/callback leaks |

### Critical: Update `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), it **must** accept the token — otherwise cancellation stalls until the call returns on its own:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If using a ManualResetEvent or similar wait:
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { _dataReady, token.WaitHandle });
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

> **If you cannot modify `GetNextItem`** (e.g., third-party code with no cancellation support), the only .NET 8 option is to move that work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` as the last resort.
